readTopic first applies the status policy, then reads and checks a non-null object with a String topic. TypeError(message) creates the chosen shape failure. The already-used body is deliberately read again to observe its contract.
```javascript
async function readTopic(response) {
  if (!response.ok) return "HTTP:" + response.status;
  const data = await response.json();
  if (data === null || typeof data !== "object" || typeof data.topic !== "string") {
    throw new TypeError("topic must be text");
  }
  return data.topic;
}
const response = new Response('{"topic":"Maps"}', {
  status: 200, headers: {"Content-Type": "application/json"}
});
console.log(await readTopic(response));
console.log(response.bodyUsed);
try { await response.json(); }
catch (error) { console.log(error instanceof TypeError); }
```
Output: Maps, true, true on separate lines. This is local Response behavior, not an HTTP server test.

For status 404 the function returns HTTP:404 without consuming the body. For a successful status with invalid JSON, the parsing rejection propagates; for valid JSON without a String topic, the explicit shape rejection propagates. Optional practice: construct a fresh response for each case. Reusing a consumed response would test body lifetime instead of the intended parsing case.
